On Wed, Jan 21, 2009 at 4:07 AM, Brett Williams <mungojelly@gmail.com>wrote:

> On Tue, Jan 20, 2009 at 5:25 PM, Luke Bergen <lukeabergen@gmail.com>
> wrote:
> > so... what is the difference between a cmene and a cmevla?
>
>
> A cmene is a name.  "Brett Williams" is a cmene, as is "se ckiku",
> though neither one has any cmevla in it.
>
> A cmevla is a category of Lojban word.  They're the only words in
> Lojban that end in consonants.
>
> So you can use a cmevla as a cmene, just as you can use a brivla as a
> selbri, but you can also use a brivla for a cmene, if you have a
> selbri cmene. :)
>

  Brett filed to mention the converse is not true.  A cmevla (like luk)
can't be used for anything but a name (either by itself when addressing
someone directly -- coi luk., or with a la in front of it when used as a
sumti -- la luk. klama lo zdani (Luke went to the house).
(Although you can convert a la+cmevla to a selbri by sticking "me" in front
of it -- la pamelas. du pa me la anderson. (Pamela is one of the Andersons)
                      --gejyspa

